<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!-- MATH 14 - Review Problems 4, online version: the sixteen problems of   -->
<!-- the Edfinity "Review Problems #4" set (Green's theorem, flux and       -->
<!-- surface integrals, and the Divergence Theorem), rebuilt as             -->
<!-- auto-graded dynamic exercises.  Same pattern as the randomized         -->
<!-- exercise in hw2-online.ptx: parameters are drawn with <de-random>,     -->
<!-- every answer box is a <fillin> graded by its own <evaluate>, so each   -->
<!-- box earns its credit separately and partial credit survives.          -->
<!--                                                                       -->
<!-- To include: add                                                       -->
<!--   <xi:include href="review4-online.ptx" />                            -->
<!-- inside the Online Assignments chapter of main.ptx.                    -->
<section xml:id="sec-exercises-review4-online" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Review Problems #4</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Sections: <xref ref="section-greens-theorem" text="title"/>,
      <xref ref="section-surface-integrals" text="title"/>, and
      <xref ref="section-divergence-theorem" text="title"/>. Work each
      problem, then type your answers into the boxes and have them checked
      here.
    </p>
    <p>
      Every answer box is graded separately, so a box you fill in correctly
      earns its credit even if another box in the same problem is wrong.
      Press <q>Check Me</q> to submit; you may check as often as you like.
    </p>
    <p>
      <term>How to type your answers.</term>
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          <term>Numbers.</term> Type a decimal number and nothing
          else<mdash/>no units, no <m>=</m> sign, no <m>\pi</m>. Give at
          least three decimal places (for instance <c>18.850</c>, not
          <c>6pi</c>). Answers are accepted within a small tolerance, so a
          little rounding is fine.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <term>Expressions.</term> Type only the expression itself. Write
          products with <c>*</c> and powers with <c>^</c>, and write
          functions with parentheses: <c>cos(u)</c>, <c>sqrt(x^2+y^2)</c>,
          <c>e^z</c>. So the expression <m>2x + e^z</m> is typed
          <c>2*x + e^z</c>. Spaces are ignored.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <term>Words.</term> Type just the word or letters asked for, in
          lower case unless the problem says otherwise.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </introduction>

  <exercises xml:id="exercises-review4-online">

  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <!-- 1.  Divergence theorem on a box  (Edfinity/RP4 #1)                   -->
  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <exercise xml:id="ex-r4o-box-flux">
    <title>Outward Flux Through the Surface of a Box</title>
    <setup>
      <de-object name="A" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="9" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="p" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="5" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="q" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="5" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="s" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="1" max="3" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="zero" context="number">
        <de-expression>0</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="divF" context="formula">
        <de-expression>2*A*x+e^z</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="flux" context="number">
        <de-expression>A*p^2*q*s+p*q*(e^s-1)</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="sumF" context="formula">
        <de-expression>A*x^2+z^4+e^z</de-expression>
      </de-object>
    </setup>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Evaluate the <alert>outward</alert> flux of the vector field
      </p>
      <me>
        \mathbf{F} = <eval obj="A"/>x^2\,\mathbf{i}
        + z^4\,\mathbf{j} + e^z\,\mathbf{k}
      </me>
      <p>
        through the surface <m>S</m> of the solid box <m>W</m> given by
        <m>0 \le x \le <eval obj="p"/></m>,
        <m>0 \le y \le <eval obj="q"/></m>,
        <m>0 \le z \le <eval obj="s"/></m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>Part A.</term> By the Divergence Theorem the flux equals
        <m>\displaystyle\int_{a}^{b}\!\int_{c}^{d}\!\int_{e}^{f}</m>
        <fillin name="b1" mode="math" ansobj="divF" width="14"/>
        <m>\,dx\,dy\,dz</m>, where
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>a =</m> <fillin name="b2" mode="number" ansobj="zero" width="4"/>,
        <m>b =</m> <fillin name="b3" mode="number" ansobj="s" width="4"/>,
        <m>c =</m> <fillin name="b4" mode="number" ansobj="zero" width="4"/>,
        <m>d =</m> <fillin name="b5" mode="number" ansobj="q" width="4"/>,
        <m>e =</m> <fillin name="b6" mode="number" ansobj="zero" width="4"/>, and
        <m>f =</m> <fillin name="b7" mode="number" ansobj="p" width="4"/>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>Part B.</term> Evaluate the integral that you set up in the
        previous part.
      </p>
      <p>
        Flux <m>=</m> <fillin name="b8" mode="number" ansobj="flux" width="12"/>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct: the integrand is the divergence,
              <m>\nabla \cdot \mathbf{F}
              = \frac{\partial}{\partial x}\left(<eval obj="A"/>x^2\right)
              + \frac{\partial}{\partial y}\left(z^4\right)
              + \frac{\partial}{\partial z}\left(e^z\right)
              = 2\!\left(<eval obj="A"/>\right)x + e^z</m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <mathcmp obj="sumF"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              That is <m>M + N + P</m>, the sum of the components of
              <m>\mathbf{F}</m>. The Divergence Theorem uses
              <m>\nabla \cdot \mathbf{F} = M_x + N_y + P_z</m>: differentiate
              each component before adding.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              The integrand is the divergence:
              <m>\nabla \cdot \mathbf{F}
              = \frac{\partial}{\partial x}\left(<eval obj="A"/>x^2\right)
              + \frac{\partial}{\partial y}\left(z^4\right)
              + \frac{\partial}{\partial z}\left(e^z\right)</m>. Note that
              the middle term is <m>0</m>, since <m>z^4</m> does not depend
              on <m>y</m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="zero.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(zero.value({})))" max="zero.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(zero.value({})))"/>
          <feedback><p>Correct.</p></feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              In <m>\int_{a}^{b}\int_{c}^{d}\int_{e}^{f}\,dx\,dy\,dz</m> the
              outermost integral pairs with <m>dz</m>, so <m>a</m> and
              <m>b</m> are the <m>z</m>-limits of the box.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="s.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(s.value({})))" max="s.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(s.value({})))"/>
          <feedback><p>Correct.</p></feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              The outermost integral pairs with <m>dz</m>, so <m>b</m> is
              the upper <m>z</m>-limit of the box.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="zero.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(zero.value({})))" max="zero.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(zero.value({})))"/>
          <feedback><p>Correct.</p></feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              The middle integral pairs with <m>dy</m>, so <m>c</m> and
              <m>d</m> are the <m>y</m>-limits of the box.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="q.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(q.value({})))" max="q.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(q.value({})))"/>
          <feedback><p>Correct.</p></feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              The middle integral pairs with <m>dy</m>, so <m>d</m> is the
              upper <m>y</m>-limit of the box.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="zero.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(zero.value({})))" max="zero.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(zero.value({})))"/>
          <feedback><p>Correct.</p></feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              The innermost integral pairs with <m>dx</m>, so <m>e</m> and
              <m>f</m> are the <m>x</m>-limits of the box.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="p.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(p.value({})))" max="p.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(p.value({})))"/>
          <feedback><p>Correct.</p></feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              The innermost integral pairs with <m>dx</m>, so <m>f</m> is
              the upper <m>x</m>-limit of the box.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="flux.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(flux.value({})))" max="flux.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(flux.value({})))"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct:
              <m>\displaystyle\int_0^{<eval obj="s"/>}\!\!
              \int_0^{<eval obj="q"/>}\!\!\int_0^{<eval obj="p"/>}
              \left(2\!\left(<eval obj="A"/>\right)x + e^z\right)
              dx\,dy\,dz
              = <eval obj="A"/>\left(<eval obj="p"/>\right)^2
              \left(<eval obj="q"/>\right)\left(<eval obj="s"/>\right)
              + \left(<eval obj="p"/>\right)\left(<eval obj="q"/>\right)
              \left(e^{<eval obj="s"/>} - 1\right)
              = <eval obj="flux"/></m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Integrate one term at a time over the box:
              <m>\int_0^{<eval obj="p"/>} 2\!\left(<eval obj="A"/>\right)
              x\,dx = <eval obj="A"/>\left(<eval obj="p"/>\right)^2</m>,
              then multiply by the lengths in <m>y</m> and <m>z</m>; and
              <m>\int_0^{<eval obj="s"/>} e^z\,dz
              = e^{<eval obj="s"/>} - 1</m>, then multiply by the lengths
              in <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>. The flux is
              <m><eval obj="flux"/></m> (to three decimals).
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
    <solution>
      <p>
        By the Divergence Theorem,
        <m>\displaystyle\iint_S \mathbf{F}\cdot\hat{\mathbf{n}}\,d\sigma
        = \iiint_W \nabla \cdot \mathbf{F}\,dV</m> with
        <m>\nabla \cdot \mathbf{F} = 2Ax + e^z</m>, where <m>A</m> is the
        coefficient of <m>x^2</m>. Writing the triple integral in the order
        <m>dx\,dy\,dz</m>, the limits are the <m>z</m>-limits outside, then
        the <m>y</m>-limits, then the <m>x</m>-limits inside. Integrating,
      </p>
      <me>
        \text{Flux} = A p^2 q s + p q \left(e^{s} - 1\right),
      </me>
      <p>
        where <m>p, q, s</m> are the <m>x</m>-, <m>y</m>- and
        <m>z</m>-dimensions of the box.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <!-- 2.  Maximizing box flux from a given divergence  (RP4 #2)            -->
  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <exercise xml:id="ex-r4o-box-div-max">
    <title>The Box With the Largest Flux</title>
    <setup>
      <de-object name="A" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="8" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="k" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="1" max="3" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="twok" context="number">
        <de-expression>2*k</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="M" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="10" max="15" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="fluxformula" context="formula">
        <de-expression>k*(2*A*a-a^2)*b*c</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="maxflux" context="number">
        <de-expression>k*A^2*M^2</de-expression>
      </de-object>
    </setup>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>\nabla \cdot \mathbf{F}
        = <eval obj="twok"/>\left(<eval obj="A"/> - x\right)</m> and
        <m>0 \le a, b, c \le <eval obj="M"/></m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        (a) Find the flux of <m>\mathbf{F}</m> out of the rectangular solid
        <m>0 \le x \le a</m>, <m>0 \le y \le b</m>, <m>0 \le z \le c</m>,
        as an expression in <m>a</m>, <m>b</m> and <m>c</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        flux <m>=</m> <fillin name="b1" mode="math" ansobj="fluxformula" width="20"/>
      </p>
      <p>
        (b) For what values of <m>a</m>, <m>b</m>, <m>c</m> is the flux
        largest?
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>a =</m> <fillin name="b2" mode="number" ansobj="A" width="6"/>,
        <m>b =</m> <fillin name="b3" mode="number" ansobj="M" width="6"/>,
        <m>c =</m> <fillin name="b4" mode="number" ansobj="M" width="6"/>
      </p>
      <p>
        (c) What is that largest flux?
      </p>
      <p>
        flux <m>=</m> <fillin name="b5" mode="number" ansobj="maxflux" width="10"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct: by the Divergence Theorem the flux is
              <m>\displaystyle\int_0^{c}\!\!\int_0^{b}\!\!\int_0^{a}
              <eval obj="twok"/>\left(<eval obj="A"/> - x\right)
              dx\,dy\,dz
              = <eval obj="k"/>\left(2\cdot<eval obj="A"/>\,a
              - a^2\right)bc</m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Use the Divergence Theorem: the flux out of the box is the
              triple integral of <m>\nabla \cdot \mathbf{F}</m> over it.
              Only <m>x</m> appears in the divergence, so
              <m>\displaystyle\int_0^{a}
              <eval obj="twok"/>\left(<eval obj="A"/> - x\right)dx
              = <eval obj="k"/>\left(2\cdot<eval obj="A"/>\,a
              - a^2\right)</m>, and the <m>y</m>- and <m>z</m>-integrals
              contribute the factor <m>bc</m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="A.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(A.value({})))" max="A.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(A.value({})))"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct: <m>a</m> should maximize
              <m>2\cdot<eval obj="A"/>\,a - a^2</m>, whose maximum is at
              <m>a = <eval obj="A"/></m>, inside the allowed interval.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp object="M" min="M.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(M.value({})))" max="M.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(M.value({})))"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Careful: unlike <m>b</m> and <m>c</m>, taking <m>a</m> as
              large as possible does not maximize the flux, because
              <m>\nabla \cdot \mathbf{F}</m> becomes negative for
              <m>x \gt <eval obj="A"/></m>: the part of the box beyond
              <m>x = <eval obj="A"/></m> subtracts flux.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Maximize <m><eval obj="k"/>\left(2\cdot<eval obj="A"/>\,a
              - a^2\right)</m> as a function of <m>a</m> on
              <m>[0, <eval obj="M"/>]</m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="M.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(M.value({})))" max="M.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(M.value({})))"/>
          <feedback><p>Correct.</p></feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              The factor <m>b</m> multiplies a positive quantity, so take
              <m>b</m> as large as allowed.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="M.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(M.value({})))" max="M.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(M.value({})))"/>
          <feedback><p>Correct.</p></feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              The factor <m>c</m> multiplies a positive quantity, so take
              <m>c</m> as large as allowed.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="maxflux.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(maxflux.value({})))" max="maxflux.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(maxflux.value({})))"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct:
              <m><eval obj="k"/>\left(2\cdot<eval obj="A"/>\cdot
              <eval obj="A"/> - \left(<eval obj="A"/>\right)^2\right)
              \left(<eval obj="M"/>\right)\left(<eval obj="M"/>\right)
              = <eval obj="maxflux"/></m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Substitute <m>a = <eval obj="A"/></m>,
              <m>b = c = <eval obj="M"/></m> into
              <m><eval obj="k"/>\left(2\cdot<eval obj="A"/>\,a
              - a^2\right)bc</m>; the result is
              <m><eval obj="maxflux"/></m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
    <solution>
      <p>
        By the Divergence Theorem the flux out of the box is
        <m>\displaystyle\int_0^{c}\!\!\int_0^{b}\!\!\int_0^{a}
        2k\left(A - x\right)dx\,dy\,dz = k\left(2Aa - a^2\right)bc</m>,
        where <m>2k</m> is the leading coefficient and <m>A</m> the root of
        the divergence. Since <m>b</m> and <m>c</m> multiply a positive
        quantity, take <m>b = c = M</m>, the largest allowed value; and
        <m>2Aa - a^2</m> is maximized at <m>a = A</m>. The largest flux is
        <m>k A^2 M^2</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <!-- 3.  Verify the Divergence Theorem on a cylinder  (RP4 #3)            -->
  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <exercise xml:id="ex-r4o-verify-cylinder">
    <title>Verifying the Divergence Theorem on a Cylinder</title>
    <setup>
      <de-object name="A" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="9" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="B" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="9" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="C" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="9" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="h" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="9" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="both" context="number">
        <de-expression>A*h*pi</de-expression>
      </de-object>
    </setup>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Verify the Divergence Theorem for the vector field and region:
      </p>
      <me>
        \mathbf{F} = \langle <eval obj="A"/>x,\ <eval obj="B"/>z,\
        <eval obj="C"/>y \rangle
      </me>
      <p>
        and the region <m>x^2 + y^2 \le 1</m>,
        <m>0 \le z \le <eval obj="h"/></m>. Give each side as a decimal
        number.
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\displaystyle\iint_{S} \mathbf{F}\cdot\hat{\mathbf{n}}\,d\sigma =</m>
        <fillin name="b1" mode="number" ansobj="both" width="12"/>
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\displaystyle\iiint_{W} \nabla \cdot \mathbf{F}\ dV =</m>
        <fillin name="b2" mode="number" ansobj="both" width="12"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="both.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(both.value({})))" max="both.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(both.value({})))"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct. On the side of the cylinder,
              <m>\hat{\mathbf{n}} = \langle \cos\theta, \sin\theta, 0\rangle</m>
              and <m>\mathbf{F}\cdot\hat{\mathbf{n}}
              = <eval obj="A"/>\cos^2\theta
              + <eval obj="B"/>z\sin\theta</m>, which integrates to
              <m><eval obj="A"/>\left(<eval obj="h"/>\right)\pi</m>; the
              integrals over the top and bottom disks vanish by symmetry.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Split <m>S</m> into the side, the top and the bottom. On the
              side, parametrized by
              <m>(\cos\theta, \sin\theta, z)</m>, the outward normal is
              <m>\langle \cos\theta, \sin\theta, 0\rangle</m> and
              <m>\mathbf{F}\cdot\hat{\mathbf{n}}
              = <eval obj="A"/>\cos^2\theta
              + <eval obj="B"/>z\sin\theta</m>. Integrating over
              <m>0 \le \theta \le 2\pi</m>,
              <m>0 \le z \le <eval obj="h"/></m> gives
              <m><eval obj="A"/>\left(<eval obj="h"/>\right)\pi
              \approx <eval obj="both"/></m>. On the top,
              <m>\mathbf{F}\cdot\mathbf{k} = <eval obj="C"/>y</m>, whose
              integral over the disk is <m>0</m> by symmetry; likewise on
              the bottom.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="both.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(both.value({})))" max="both.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(both.value({})))"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct: <m>\nabla \cdot \mathbf{F}
              = <eval obj="A"/></m>, a constant, so the triple integral is
              <m><eval obj="A"/>\cdot
              \text{Vol}(W) = <eval obj="A"/>\cdot \pi \cdot 1^2 \cdot
              <eval obj="h"/> \approx <eval obj="both"/></m>, matching the
              surface integral, as the Divergence Theorem promises.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Here <m>\nabla \cdot \mathbf{F}
              = \frac{\partial}{\partial x}\left(<eval obj="A"/>x\right)
              + \frac{\partial}{\partial y}\left(<eval obj="B"/>z\right)
              + \frac{\partial}{\partial z}\left(<eval obj="C"/>y\right)
              = <eval obj="A"/></m>, so the integral is
              <m><eval obj="A"/></m> times the volume
              <m>\pi \cdot 1^2 \cdot <eval obj="h"/></m> of the cylinder.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
    <solution>
      <p>
        With <m>\mathbf{F} = \langle Ax, Bz, Cy\rangle</m> we get
        <m>\nabla \cdot \mathbf{F} = A</m>, so
        <m>\iiint_W \nabla \cdot \mathbf{F}\,dV
        = A\,\text{Vol}(W) = A\pi h</m>. Computing the surface integral
        directly: on the side, <m>\mathbf{F}\cdot\hat{\mathbf{n}}
        = A\cos^2\theta + Bz\sin\theta</m> integrates to <m>A h\pi</m>; the
        top and bottom disks contribute <m>0</m> by symmetry, since there
        <m>\mathbf{F}\cdot\hat{\mathbf{n}} = \pm Cy</m>. Both sides equal
        <m>A h \pi</m>, verifying the theorem.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <!-- 4.  Divergence theorem on a sphere  (RP4 #4)                         -->
  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <exercise xml:id="ex-r4o-sphere">
    <title>Flux Through a Sphere</title>
    <setup>
      <de-object name="R" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="5" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="Rsq" context="number">
        <de-expression>R^2</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="c" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="1" max="9" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="divF" context="formula">
        <de-expression>3*x^2+3*z^2</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="flux" context="number">
        <de-expression>8*pi*R^5/5</de-expression>
      </de-object>
    </setup>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Use the Divergence Theorem to evaluate the surface integral
        <m>\displaystyle\iint_{S} \mathbf{F}\cdot\hat{\mathbf{n}}\,d\sigma</m>, where
      </p>
      <me>
        \mathbf{F} = \langle x^3,\ <eval obj="c"/>,\ z^3 \rangle
      </me>
      <p>
        and <m>S</m> is the sphere
        <m>x^2 + y^2 + z^2 = <eval obj="Rsq"/></m>, oriented outward.
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\nabla \cdot \mathbf{F} =</m>
        <fillin name="b1" mode="math" ansobj="divF" width="14"/>
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\displaystyle\iint_{S} \mathbf{F}\cdot\hat{\mathbf{n}}\,d\sigma =</m>
        <fillin name="b2" mode="number" ansobj="flux" width="12"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct: the constant middle component differentiates to
              zero, leaving <m>3x^2 + 3z^2</m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              <m>\nabla \cdot \mathbf{F}
              = \frac{\partial}{\partial x}(x^3)
              + \frac{\partial}{\partial y}\left(<eval obj="c"/>\right)
              + \frac{\partial}{\partial z}(z^3) = 3x^2 + 3z^2</m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="flux.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(flux.value({})))" max="flux.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(flux.value({})))"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct: in spherical coordinates
              <m>x^2 + z^2 = \rho^2\left(1 -
              \sin^2\phi\,\sin^2\theta\right)</m>, and the integral over
              the ball of radius <m><eval obj="R"/></m> works out to
              <m>\dfrac{8\pi\left(<eval obj="R"/>\right)^5}{5}
              \approx <eval obj="flux"/></m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Convert
              <m>\iiint_W 3\left(x^2 + z^2\right)dV</m> to spherical
              coordinates. Because the integrand omits <m>y^2</m>, use
              <m>x^2 + z^2 = \rho^2\left(1 -
              \sin^2\phi\,\sin^2\theta\right)</m>; then
              <m>\displaystyle 3\int_0^{2\pi}\!\!\int_0^{\pi}\!\!
              \int_0^{<eval obj="R"/>} \rho^4\left(\sin\phi -
              \sin^3\phi\,\sin^2\theta\right)d\rho\,d\phi\,d\theta
              = \frac{8\pi\left(<eval obj="R"/>\right)^5}{5}
              \approx <eval obj="flux"/></m>. (A slicker route: by
              symmetry the integrals of <m>x^2</m>, <m>y^2</m>, <m>z^2</m>
              over the ball are equal, each
              <m>\tfrac13\int \rho^2\,dV = \tfrac{4\pi R^5}{15}</m>, so the
              flux is <m>6\cdot\tfrac{4\pi R^5}{15}</m>.)
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
    <solution>
      <p>
        <m>\nabla \cdot \mathbf{F} = 3x^2 + 3z^2</m>. By symmetry of the
        ball <m>W</m> of radius <m>R</m>, the integrals of <m>x^2</m>,
        <m>y^2</m>, <m>z^2</m> over <m>W</m> are all equal to
        <m>\frac13\iiint_W \rho^2\,dV
        = \frac13\int_0^{2\pi}\!\!\int_0^{\pi}\!\!\int_0^{R}
        \rho^4\sin\phi\,d\rho\,d\phi\,d\theta = \frac{4\pi R^5}{15}</m>.
        Hence
      </p>
      <me>
        \iint_S \mathbf{F}\cdot\hat{\mathbf{n}}\,d\sigma
        = \iiint_W 3\left(x^2 + z^2\right)dV
        = 6\cdot\frac{4\pi R^5}{15} = \frac{8\pi R^5}{5}.
      </me>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <!-- 5.  Flux through a paraboloid cap  (RP4 #5)                          -->
  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <exercise xml:id="ex-r4o-paraboloid-cap">
    <title>Flux Through a Paraboloid Cap</title>
    <setup>
      <de-object name="a" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="1" max="2" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="u" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="0" max="1" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="t" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="1" max="4" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="b" context="number">
        <de-expression>(2*u-1)*t</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="h" context="number">
        <de-expression>1+a^2</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="flux" context="number">
        <de-expression>b*(a^4/2+a^2)*pi</de-expression>
      </de-object>
    </setup>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let
        <m>\mathbf{F}(x,y,z) = z\tan\!\left(y^2\right)\mathbf{i}
        + z^3\ln\!\left(x^2+1\right)\mathbf{j}
        + <eval obj="b"/>z\,\mathbf{k}</m>. Use the Divergence Theorem to
        find the flux of <m>\mathbf{F}</m> across the part of the
        paraboloid <m>x^2 + y^2 + z = <eval obj="h"/></m> that lies above
        the plane <m>z = 1</m> and is oriented upward.
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\nabla \cdot \mathbf{F} =</m>
        <fillin name="b1" mode="number" ansobj="b" width="6"/>
        (a number)
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\displaystyle\iint_{S} \mathbf{F}\cdot\hat{\mathbf{n}}\,d\sigma =</m>
        <fillin name="b2" mode="number" ansobj="flux" width="12"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="b.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(b.value({})))" max="b.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(b.value({})))"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct: the first component does not involve <m>x</m> and
              the second does not involve <m>y</m>, so only the third
              contributes: <m>\nabla \cdot \mathbf{F}
              = <eval obj="b"/></m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Differentiate each component with respect to its own
              variable: <m>\frac{\partial}{\partial x}\left(
              z\tan\!\left(y^2\right)\right) = 0</m>,
              <m>\frac{\partial}{\partial y}\left(z^3\ln\!\left(x^2+1
              \right)\right) = 0</m>, and
              <m>\frac{\partial}{\partial z}\left(<eval obj="b"/>z\right)
              = <eval obj="b"/></m>. The scary-looking terms are decoys.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="flux.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(flux.value({})))" max="flux.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(flux.value({})))"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct. Closing the surface with the downward disk
              <m>x^2 + y^2 \le \left(<eval obj="a"/>\right)^2</m> at
              <m>z = 1</m> and applying the Divergence Theorem gives flux
              <m>= <eval obj="b"/>\left(\tfrac{1}{2}
              \left(<eval obj="a"/>\right)^4 +
              \left(<eval obj="a"/>\right)^2\right)\pi
              \approx <eval obj="flux"/></m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              The surface is not closed, so first close it: let <m>S_1</m>
              be the disk <m>x^2 + y^2 \le \left(<eval obj="a"/>\right)^2</m>
              in the plane <m>z = 1</m>, oriented downward. On <m>S_1</m>,
              <m>\mathbf{F}\cdot(-\mathbf{k}) = -<eval obj="b"/>z
              = -<eval obj="b"/></m>, so
              <m>\iint_{S_1}\mathbf{F}\cdot\hat{\mathbf{n}}\,d\sigma
              = -<eval obj="b"/>\pi\left(<eval obj="a"/>\right)^2</m>.
              The closed surface encloses the region between the plane and
              the paraboloid, whose volume is
              <m>\tfrac{\pi}{2}\left(<eval obj="a"/>\right)^4</m>, so the
              total outward flux is <m><eval obj="b"/>\cdot
              \tfrac{\pi}{2}\left(<eval obj="a"/>\right)^4</m>.
              Subtracting the disk's share,
              <m>\iint_{S}\mathbf{F}\cdot\hat{\mathbf{n}}\,d\sigma
              = <eval obj="b"/>\left(\tfrac{1}{2}
              \left(<eval obj="a"/>\right)^4
              + \left(<eval obj="a"/>\right)^2\right)\pi
              \approx <eval obj="flux"/></m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
    <solution>
      <p>
        Write <m>b</m> for the coefficient of <m>z\,\mathbf{k}</m> and
        <m>a^2 = h - 1</m>, so the paraboloid meets the plane <m>z=1</m>
        in the circle of radius <m>a</m>. Then
        <m>\nabla \cdot \mathbf{F} = b</m>. Close the surface with the
        disk <m>S_1</m> of radius <m>a</m> at <m>z = 1</m>, oriented
        downward; there <m>\mathbf{F}\cdot(-\mathbf{k}) = -b</m>, so
        <m>\iint_{S_1} = -b\pi a^2</m>. The enclosed volume is
        <m>\int_0^{2\pi}\!\!\int_0^{a}\left(a^2 - r^2\right)r\,dr\,d\theta
        = \tfrac{\pi a^4}{2}</m>, so the closed-surface flux is
        <m>b\,\tfrac{\pi a^4}{2}</m>, and
      </p>
      <me>
        \iint_S \mathbf{F}\cdot\hat{\mathbf{n}}\,d\sigma
        = b\,\frac{\pi a^4}{2} - \left(-b\pi a^2\right)
        = b\left(\frac{a^4}{2} + a^2\right)\pi.
      </me>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <!-- 6.  Radial field through a cylinder on the x-axis  (RP4 #6)          -->
  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <exercise xml:id="ex-r4o-cylinder-x-axis">
    <title>Radial Field Through a Closed Cylinder</title>
    <setup>
      <de-object name="R" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="6" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="L" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="1" max="4" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="three" context="number">
        <de-expression>3</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="flux" context="number">
        <de-expression>6*pi*R^2*L</de-expression>
      </de-object>
    </setup>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Compute the flux of the vector field
        <m>\mathbf{F} = x\,\mathbf{i} + y\,\mathbf{j} + z\,\mathbf{k}</m>
        through the surface <m>S</m>, which is a closed cylinder of radius
        <m><eval obj="R"/></m> centered on the <m>x</m>-axis, with
        <m>-<eval obj="L"/> \le x \le <eval obj="L"/></m>, and oriented
        outward.
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\nabla \cdot \mathbf{F} =</m>
        <fillin name="b1" mode="number" ansobj="three" width="6"/>
        (a number)
      </p>
      <p>
        Flux <m>=</m> <fillin name="b2" mode="number" ansobj="flux" width="12"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="three.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(three.value({})))" max="three.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(three.value({})))"/>
          <feedback><p>Correct: <m>1 + 1 + 1 = 3</m>.</p></feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              <m>\nabla \cdot \mathbf{F}
              = \frac{\partial x}{\partial x}
              + \frac{\partial y}{\partial y}
              + \frac{\partial z}{\partial z} = 3</m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="flux.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(flux.value({})))" max="flux.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(flux.value({})))"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct: the cylinder is a closed surface, so the flux is
              <m>3\cdot\text{Vol}
              = 3\,\pi\left(<eval obj="R"/>\right)^2
              \left(2\cdot<eval obj="L"/>\right)
              \approx <eval obj="flux"/></m>. It does not matter that the
              cylinder's axis is the <m>x</m>-axis rather than the
              <m>z</m>-axis.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Since <m>S</m> is closed, apply the Divergence Theorem:
              flux <m>= \iiint_W 3\,dV = 3\cdot\text{Vol}(W)</m>. The
              cylinder has radius <m><eval obj="R"/></m> and length
              <m>2\cdot<eval obj="L"/></m> along the <m>x</m>-axis, so its
              volume is <m>\pi\left(<eval obj="R"/>\right)^2\cdot
              2\cdot<eval obj="L"/></m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
    <solution>
      <p>
        The surface is closed, so by the Divergence Theorem with
        <m>\nabla \cdot \mathbf{F} = 3</m>,
        <m>\text{Flux} = 3\,\text{Vol}(W) = 3\,\pi R^2 (2L) = 6\pi R^2 L</m>,
        where <m>R</m> is the radius and <m>2L</m> the length of the
        cylinder.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <!-- 7.  Constant field through a helicoid  (RP4 #7)                      -->
  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <exercise xml:id="ex-r4o-helicoid">
    <title>Flux Through a Helicoid</title>
    <setup>
      <de-object name="w" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="1" max="4" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="c" context="number">
        <de-expression>w/2</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="integrand" context="formula">
        <de-expression>(w/2)*(sin(u)-cos(u)+v)</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="flux" context="number">
        <de-expression>(w/2)*(2+pi/2)</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="wrongsign" context="formula">
        <de-expression>(w/2)*(cos(u)-sin(u)-v)</de-expression>
      </de-object>
    </setup>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Evaluate
        <m>\displaystyle\iint_S \mathbf{F}\cdot\hat{\mathbf{n}}\,d\sigma</m>,
        where the vector field
        <m>\mathbf{F} = <eval obj="c"/>\,\mathbf{i}
        + <eval obj="c"/>\,\mathbf{j} + <eval obj="c"/>\,\mathbf{k}</m>
        and the parametrized surface <m>S</m> is the helicoid
      </p>
      <me>
        \mathbf{r}(u,v) = \left(v\cos u,\ v\sin u,\ u\right),
        \qquad 0 \le u \le \pi, \quad 0 \le v \le 1,
      </me>
      <p>
        oriented upward.
      </p>
      <p>
        First compute the integrand (as an expression in <m>u</m> and
        <m>v</m>):
        <m>\mathbf{F}\cdot\left(\mathbf{r}_v\times\mathbf{r}_u\right) =</m>
        <fillin name="b1" mode="math" ansobj="integrand" width="20"/>
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\displaystyle\iint_S \mathbf{F}\cdot\hat{\mathbf{n}}\,d\sigma =</m>
        <fillin name="b2" mode="number" ansobj="flux" width="12"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct:
              <m>\mathbf{r}_v\times\mathbf{r}_u
              = \sin u\,\mathbf{i} - \cos u\,\mathbf{j} + v\,\mathbf{k}</m>
              (this is the upward choice, since the
              <m>\mathbf{k}</m>-component <m>v</m> is nonnegative), and
              dotting with <m>\mathbf{F}</m> gives
              <m><eval obj="c"/>\left(\sin u - \cos u + v\right)</m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <mathcmp obj="wrongsign"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              That is the opposite sign: you used
              <m>\mathbf{r}_u\times\mathbf{r}_v</m>, whose
              <m>\mathbf{k}</m>-component is <m>-v \le 0</m>, the downward
              orientation. The problem asks for the upward one.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Compute <m>\mathbf{r}_u = \left(-v\sin u,\ v\cos u,\
              1\right)</m> and <m>\mathbf{r}_v = \left(\cos u,\ \sin u,\
              0\right)</m>; then
              <m>\mathbf{r}_v\times\mathbf{r}_u
              = \sin u\,\mathbf{i} - \cos u\,\mathbf{j} +
              v\,\mathbf{k}</m>, and
              <m>\mathbf{F}\cdot\left(\mathbf{r}_v\times\mathbf{r}_u\right)
              = <eval obj="c"/>\left(\sin u - \cos u + v\right)</m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="flux.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(flux.value({})))" max="flux.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(flux.value({})))"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct:
              <m><eval obj="c"/>\displaystyle\int_0^1\!\!\int_0^{\pi}
              \left(\sin u - \cos u + v\right)du\,dv
              = <eval obj="c"/>\left(2 + \frac{\pi}{2}\right)
              \approx <eval obj="flux"/></m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Integrate over the parameter rectangle:
              <m>\int_0^{\pi}\left(\sin u - \cos u\right)du = 2</m> and
              <m>\int_0^1 \pi v\,dv = \frac{\pi}{2}</m>, so the flux is
              <m><eval obj="c"/>\left(2 + \frac{\pi}{2}\right)
              \approx <eval obj="flux"/></m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
    <solution>
      <p>
        With <m>\mathbf{r}_u = \left(-v\sin u, v\cos u, 1\right)</m> and
        <m>\mathbf{r}_v = \left(\cos u, \sin u, 0\right)</m>, the upward
        normal is
        <m>\mathbf{r}_v\times\mathbf{r}_u
        = \sin u\,\mathbf{i} - \cos u\,\mathbf{j} + v\,\mathbf{k}</m>.
        Writing <m>c</m> for the common component of <m>\mathbf{F}</m>,
      </p>
      <me>
        \iint_S \mathbf{F}\cdot\hat{\mathbf{n}}\,d\sigma
        = c\int_0^1\!\!\int_0^{\pi}\left(\sin u - \cos u + v\right)
        du\,dv = c\left(2 + \frac{\pi}{2}\right).
      </me>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <!-- 8.  Flux through a paraboloid over a disk  (RP4 #8)                  -->
  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <exercise xml:id="ex-r4o-paraboloid-updisk">
    <title>Flux Through a Paraboloid, Directly</title>
    <setup>
      <de-object name="a" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="1" max="5" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="R" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="6" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="Rsq" context="number">
        <de-expression>R^2</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="integrand" context="formula">
        <de-expression>2*a*(x^2+y^2)</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="flux" context="number">
        <de-expression>pi*a*R^4</de-expression>
      </de-object>
    </setup>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Compute the flux of the vector field
        <m>\mathbf{F} = <eval obj="a"/>x\,\mathbf{i}
        + <eval obj="a"/>y\,\mathbf{j}</m> through the surface <m>S</m>,
        which is the part of the surface
        <m>z = <eval obj="Rsq"/> - \left(x^2 + y^2\right)</m> above the
        disk of radius <m><eval obj="R"/></m> centered at the origin,
        oriented <alert>upward</alert>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Writing <m>z = f(x,y)</m>, we have
        <m>\hat{\mathbf{n}}\,d\sigma = \left(-f_x\,\mathbf{i} - f_y\,\mathbf{j}
        + \mathbf{k}\right)dx\,dy</m>, so (as an expression in <m>x</m>
        and <m>y</m>)
        <m>\mathbf{F}\cdot\left(-f_x\,\mathbf{i} - f_y\,\mathbf{j}
        + \mathbf{k}\right) =</m>
        <fillin name="b1" mode="math" ansobj="integrand" width="16"/>
      </p>
      <p>
        Flux <m>=</m> <fillin name="b2" mode="number" ansobj="flux" width="12"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct: <m>-f_x = 2x</m>, <m>-f_y = 2y</m>, and
              <m>\mathbf{F}</m> has no <m>\mathbf{k}</m>-component, so the
              dot product is <m>2\!\left(<eval obj="a"/>\right)
              \left(x^2 + y^2\right)</m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              With <m>f(x,y) = <eval obj="Rsq"/> - x^2 - y^2</m> we get
              <m>-f_x = 2x</m> and <m>-f_y = 2y</m>, so
              <m>\mathbf{F}\cdot\left(2x\,\mathbf{i} + 2y\,\mathbf{j} +
              \mathbf{k}\right)
              = 2\!\left(<eval obj="a"/>\right)x^2
              + 2\!\left(<eval obj="a"/>\right)y^2</m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="flux.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(flux.value({})))" max="flux.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(flux.value({})))"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct: in polar coordinates,
              <m>\displaystyle\int_0^{2\pi}\!\!\int_0^{<eval obj="R"/>}
              2\!\left(<eval obj="a"/>\right)r^2\cdot r\,dr\,d\theta
              = \pi\left(<eval obj="a"/>\right)
              \left(<eval obj="R"/>\right)^4 \approx <eval obj="flux"/></m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Convert to polar coordinates:
              <m>x^2 + y^2 = r^2</m> and <m>dA = r\,dr\,d\theta</m>, so
              the flux is
              <m>\int_0^{2\pi}\!\!\int_0^{<eval obj="R"/>}
              2\!\left(<eval obj="a"/>\right)r^3\,dr\,d\theta
              = 2\pi\cdot 2\!\left(<eval obj="a"/>\right)
              \frac{\left(<eval obj="R"/>\right)^4}{4}
              = \pi\left(<eval obj="a"/>\right)
              \left(<eval obj="R"/>\right)^4</m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
    <solution>
      <p>
        Writing the surface as <m>z = f(x,y) = R^2 - x^2 - y^2</m> over
        the disk <m>D</m> of radius <m>R</m>,
        <m>\hat{\mathbf{n}}\,d\sigma = \left(2x\,\mathbf{i} + 2y\,\mathbf{j} +
        \mathbf{k}\right)dx\,dy</m> and
        <m>\mathbf{F}\cdot\hat{\mathbf{n}}\,d\sigma = 2a\left(x^2+y^2\right)dx\,dy</m>,
        where <m>a</m> is the coefficient in <m>\mathbf{F}</m>. In polar
        coordinates,
      </p>
      <me>
        \text{Flux} = \int_0^{2\pi}\!\!\int_0^{R} 2a\,r^3\,dr\,d\theta
        = \pi a R^4.
      </me>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <!-- 9.  Green's theorem on a clockwise parallelogram  (RP4 #9)           -->
  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <figure xml:id="fig-r4o-parallelogram">
    <caption>
      The parallelogram of <xref ref="ex-r4o-parallelogram"/>, with its
      orientation. Note the direction of the arrows.
    </caption>
    <image width="55%">
      <shortdescription>
      A parallelogram anchored at the origin with arrows showing its boundary orientation.
      </shortdescription>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-r4o-parallelogram">
        <diagram dimensions="(360,240)" margins="10">
          <coordinates bbox="(-0.8,-0.55,4.9,2.3)">
            <axes at="pg-axes" xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="no" arrows="1"/>
            <polygon at="pg-par" closed="yes" stroke="#CC0079" thickness="3"
                fill="#CC0079" fill-opacity="0.15"
                points="((0,0),(2,1.4),(4,1.4),(2,0))"/>
            <vector at="pg-e1" v="(0.7,0.49)" tail="(0.75,0.525)"
                stroke="#CC0079" thickness="3"/>
            <vector at="pg-e2" v="(0.8,0)" tail="(2.7,1.4)"
                stroke="#CC0079" thickness="3"/>
            <vector at="pg-e3" v="(-0.7,-0.49)" tail="(3.35,0.945)"
                stroke="#CC0079" thickness="3"/>
            <vector at="pg-e4" v="(-0.8,0)" tail="(1.3,0)"
                stroke="#CC0079" thickness="3"/>
            <point at="pg-O" p="(0,0)" fill="black" alignment="sw">
              <m>(0,0)</m>
            </point>
            <point at="pg-B" p="(2,0)" fill="black" alignment="se">
              <m>(x_0,0)</m>
            </point>
            <point at="pg-T1" p="(2,1.4)" fill="black" alignment="nw">
              <m>(x_0,y_0)</m>
            </point>
            <point at="pg-T2" p="(4,1.4)" fill="black" alignment="ne">
              <m>(2x_0,y_0)</m>
            </point>
          </coordinates>

          <annotations>
            <annotation ref="figure" text="A parallelogram with vertices at the origin, at x nought comma zero, at two x nought comma y nought, and at x nought comma y nought. Arrows on the edges show the boundary traversed clockwise: up the left edge from the origin, rightward across the top, down the right edge, and leftward along the bottom back to the origin.">
              <annotation ref="pg-par" text="The parallelogram with vertices at the origin, x nought comma zero, two x nought comma y nought, and x nought comma y nought."/>
              <annotation ref="pg-e1" text="An arrow on the left edge pointing up and to the right, from the origin toward x nought comma y nought."/>
              <annotation ref="pg-e2" text="An arrow on the top edge pointing to the right."/>
              <annotation ref="pg-e3" text="An arrow on the right edge pointing down and to the left, toward x nought comma zero."/>
              <annotation ref="pg-e4" text="An arrow on the bottom edge pointing to the left, back toward the origin. Altogether the circulation is clockwise."/>
            </annotation>
          </annotations>
        </diagram>
      </prefigure>
    </image>
  </figure>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-r4o-parallelogram">
    <title>Work Around a Parallelogram</title>
    <setup>
      <de-object name="n" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="6" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="A" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="9" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="x0" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="5" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="y0" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="5" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="curl" context="number">
        <de-expression>A</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="area" context="number">
        <de-expression>x0*y0</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="work" context="number">
        <de-expression>-A*x0*y0</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="poswork" context="number">
        <de-expression>A*x0*y0</de-expression>
      </de-object>
    </setup>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Use Green's Theorem to evaluate the work done by
        <m>\mathbf{F} = \langle x^{<eval obj="n"/>},\
        <eval obj="A"/>x \rangle</m> in moving an object around the
        boundary of the parallelogram in
        <xref ref="fig-r4o-parallelogram"/>
        (<alert>note the orientation</alert>), with
        <m>x_0 = <eval obj="x0"/></m> and <m>y_0 = <eval obj="y0"/></m>.
        Assume that the object circulates only once around the boundary
        and that its motion begins at the origin and ends at the same
        point.
      </p>
      <p>
        With <m>\mathbf{F} = M\,\mathbf{i} + N\,\mathbf{j}</m>, first
        compute (a number):
        <m>\dfrac{\partial N}{\partial x}
        - \dfrac{\partial M}{\partial y} =</m>
        <fillin name="b1" mode="number" ansobj="curl" width="6"/>
      </p>
      <p>
        The area of the parallelogram is
        <fillin name="b2" mode="number" ansobj="area" width="6"/>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Work <m>=</m> <fillin name="b3" mode="number" ansobj="work" width="10"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="curl.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(curl.value({})))" max="curl.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(curl.value({})))"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct:
              <m>\frac{\partial}{\partial x}\left(<eval obj="A"/>x\right)
              - \frac{\partial}{\partial y}\left(x^{<eval obj="n"/>}\right)
              = <eval obj="A"/> - 0</m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Here <m>M = x^{<eval obj="n"/>}</m> depends only on
              <m>x</m>, so <m>\frac{\partial M}{\partial y} = 0</m>, and
              <m>\frac{\partial N}{\partial x}
              = <eval obj="A"/></m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="area.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(area.value({})))" max="area.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(area.value({})))"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct: base <m>x_0</m> and height <m>y_0</m>, so the area
              is <m>x_0 y_0 = <eval obj="area"/></m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              A parallelogram's area is base times height. The base along
              the <m>x</m>-axis runs from <m>(0,0)</m> to
              <m>\left(x_0, 0\right)</m>, and the height is <m>y_0</m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="work.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(work.value({})))" max="work.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(work.value({})))"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct. Green's Theorem gives
              <m>\oint \mathbf{F}\cdot d\mathbf{r}
              = <eval obj="A"/>\cdot\text{Area}</m> for the
              <em>counterclockwise</em> orientation; since the figure's
              arrows run clockwise, the work is
              <m>-<eval obj="A"/>\cdot<eval obj="area"/>
              = <eval obj="work"/></m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp object="poswork" min="poswork.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(poswork.value({})))" max="poswork.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(poswork.value({})))"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Check the orientation: the arrows in the figure run
              <em>clockwise</em>, and Green's Theorem as stated applies to
              the counterclockwise orientation. Your answer is off by a
              sign.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              By Green's Theorem the counterclockwise circulation equals
              <m>\iint_R\left(N_x - M_y\right)dA
              = <eval obj="A"/>\cdot\text{Area}
              = <eval obj="A"/>\cdot<eval obj="area"/></m>. The figure's
              orientation is clockwise, so flip the sign:
              Work <m>= <eval obj="work"/></m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
    <solution>
      <p>
        With <m>M = x^{n}</m> and <m>N = Ax</m>,
        <m>N_x - M_y = A</m>. For the counterclockwise orientation
        Green's Theorem would give <m>A\cdot\text{Area} = A x_0 y_0</m>;
        the boundary in the figure is traversed clockwise, so
        <m>\text{Work} = -A x_0 y_0</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <!-- 10.  Three gradient fields and the unit circle  (RP4 #10)            -->
  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <exercise xml:id="ex-r4o-three-fields">
    <title>Three Gradient Fields and the Unit Circle</title>
    <setup>
      <de-object name="a1" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="1" max="5" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="a2" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="1" max="5" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="a3" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="1" max="5" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="fpot" context="formula">
        <de-expression>a1*x*y</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="hpot" context="formula">
        <de-expression>a3*sqrt(x^2+y^2)</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="zero" context="number">
        <de-expression>0</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="Gint" context="number">
        <de-expression>-2*pi*a2</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="posGint" context="number">
        <de-expression>2*pi*a2</de-expression>
      </de-object>
    </setup>
    <statement>
      <p>
        (a) Each of the vector fields
      </p>
      <md>
        <mrow>
          \mathbf{F} \amp = <eval obj="a1"/>y\,\mathbf{i}
          + <eval obj="a1"/>x\,\mathbf{j},
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \mathbf{G} \amp = \frac{<eval obj="a2"/>y}{x^2+y^2}\,\mathbf{i}
          + \frac{-<eval obj="a2"/>x}{x^2+y^2}\,\mathbf{j},
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \mathbf{H} \amp = \frac{<eval obj="a3"/>x}{\sqrt{x^2+y^2}}
          \,\mathbf{i}
          + \frac{<eval obj="a3"/>y}{\sqrt{x^2+y^2}}\,\mathbf{j}
        </mrow>
      </md>
      <p>
        is a gradient vector field on some domain (not necessarily the
        whole plane). Find potential functions for <m>\mathbf{F}</m> and
        <m>\mathbf{H}</m>, choosing the constant so that the potential is
        <m>0</m> at the origin; and find the constant <m>C</m> for the
        potential of <m>\mathbf{G}</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        For <m>\mathbf{F}</m>: <m>f(x,y) =</m>
        <fillin name="b1" mode="math" ansobj="fpot" width="16"/>
      </p>
      <p>
        For <m>\mathbf{G}</m>: <m>g(x,y)
        = C\arctan\!\left(\dfrac{x}{y}\right)</m> with <m>C =</m>
        <fillin name="b2" mode="number" ansobj="a2" width="6"/>
      </p>
      <p>
        For <m>\mathbf{H}</m>: <m>h(x,y) =</m>
        <fillin name="b3" mode="math" ansobj="hpot" width="16"/>
      </p>
      <p>
        (b) Find the line integrals of <m>\mathbf{F}</m>,
        <m>\mathbf{G}</m>, <m>\mathbf{H}</m> around the curve <m>C</m>
        given to be the unit circle in the <m>xy</m>-plane, centered at
        the origin, and traversed counterclockwise.
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\displaystyle\int_C \mathbf{F}\cdot d\mathbf{r} =</m>
        <fillin name="b4" mode="number" ansobj="zero" width="8"/>,
        <m>\displaystyle\int_C \mathbf{G}\cdot d\mathbf{r} =</m>
        <fillin name="b5" mode="number" ansobj="Gint" width="8"/>,
        <m>\displaystyle\int_C \mathbf{H}\cdot d\mathbf{r} =</m>
        <fillin name="b6" mode="number" ansobj="zero" width="8"/>
      </p>
      <p>
        (c) For which of the three vector fields can Green's Theorem be
        used to calculate the line integral in part (b)? Type the letters
        of all that apply, with no spaces (for example <c>fg</c>):
        <fillin name="b7" mode="string" answer="f" width="6"/>
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>(Be sure that you are able to explain why or why not.)</em>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct: <m>\nabla\!\left(<eval obj="a1"/>xy\right)
              = <eval obj="a1"/>y\,\mathbf{i}
              + <eval obj="a1"/>x\,\mathbf{j} = \mathbf{F}</m>, for all
              <m>(x,y)</m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Look for <m>f</m> with <m>f_x = <eval obj="a1"/>y</m> and
              <m>f_y = <eval obj="a1"/>x</m>; integrating the first with
              respect to <m>x</m> suggests
              <m>f = <eval obj="a1"/>xy + g(y)</m>, and the second
              equation forces <m>g</m> to be constant.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="a2.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(a2.value({})))" max="a2.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(a2.value({})))"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct: <m>\frac{\partial}{\partial x}\left(
              <eval obj="a2"/>\arctan\!\left(\tfrac{x}{y}\right)\right)
              = \frac{<eval obj="a2"/>y}{x^2+y^2}</m> and
              <m>\frac{\partial}{\partial y}\left(
              <eval obj="a2"/>\arctan\!\left(\tfrac{x}{y}\right)\right)
              = \frac{-<eval obj="a2"/>x}{x^2+y^2}</m>, wherever
              <m>y \ne 0</m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Differentiate <m>C\arctan\!\left(\tfrac{x}{y}\right)</m>
              with respect to <m>x</m>:
              <m>\frac{C/y}{1 + x^2/y^2} = \frac{Cy}{x^2+y^2}</m>. Match
              the coefficient with the first component of
              <m>\mathbf{G}</m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct:
              <m>\nabla\!\left(<eval obj="a3"/>\sqrt{x^2+y^2}\right)
              = \frac{<eval obj="a3"/>x}{\sqrt{x^2+y^2}}\,\mathbf{i}
              + \frac{<eval obj="a3"/>y}{\sqrt{x^2+y^2}}\,\mathbf{j}
              = \mathbf{H}</m>, except at the origin.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              <m>\mathbf{H}</m> is a radial field of constant magnitude
              <m><eval obj="a3"/></m>; try a potential of the form
              (constant)<m>\cdot\sqrt{x^2+y^2}</m> and differentiate to
              fix the constant.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="zero.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(zero.value({})))" max="zero.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(zero.value({})))"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct: <m>\mathbf{F}</m> is a gradient field on the whole
              plane, and <m>C</m> is closed, so the integral is <m>0</m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              <m>\mathbf{F} = \nabla f</m> with <m>f</m> defined on all of
              <m>\mathbb{R}^2</m>, and the circle is a closed curve: the
              fundamental theorem of line integrals gives <m>0</m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="Gint.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(Gint.value({})))" max="Gint.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(Gint.value({})))"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct: on the unit circle <m>\mathbf{G}</m> is tangent to
              the circle, of length <m><eval obj="a2"/></m>, pointing
              <em>against</em> the counterclockwise direction, so the
              integral is <m>-<eval obj="a2"/>\cdot 2\pi</m>. The potential
              <m>g</m> does not help here: it is not defined on the whole
              circle (it fails at <m>y = 0</m>).
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp object="posGint" min="posGint.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(posGint.value({})))" max="posGint.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(posGint.value({})))"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Right size, wrong sign: on the unit circle
              <m>\mathbf{G} = <eval obj="a2"/>\left(y, -x\right)</m>
              points <em>clockwise</em>, against the direction of travel.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Parametrize with <m>x = \cos t</m>, <m>y = \sin t</m>: on
              the circle <m>x^2 + y^2 = 1</m>, so
              <m>\mathbf{G} = <eval obj="a2"/>\left(\sin t, -\cos t
              \right)</m> while
              <m>d\mathbf{r} = \left(-\sin t, \cos t\right)dt</m>; the dot
              product is <m>-<eval obj="a2"/></m>, and integrating over
              <m>0 \le t \le 2\pi</m> gives
              <m>-2\pi\cdot<eval obj="a2"/></m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="zero.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(zero.value({})))" max="zero.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(zero.value({})))"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct: <m>\mathbf{H}</m> points radially outward, so it is
              perpendicular to the circle everywhere, and the integral is
              <m>0</m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              <m>\mathbf{H}</m> is radial and the circle is traversed
              tangentially, so <m>\mathbf{H}\cdot d\mathbf{r} = 0</m> at
              every point.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <strcmp case="insensitive">f</strcmp>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct: Green's Theorem needs the field to be defined and
              differentiable everywhere inside the curve.
              <m>\mathbf{G}</m> and <m>\mathbf{H}</m> are undefined at
              the origin, which lies inside the unit circle, so the
              theorem applies only to <m>\mathbf{F}</m> (and indeed
              <m>N_x - M_y = <eval obj="a1"/> - <eval obj="a1"/> = 0</m>
              gives the answer <m>0</m> instantly).
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <strcmp case="insensitive">fgh</strcmp>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Careful: Green's Theorem requires <m>M</m> and <m>N</m> to
              have continuous partials on <em>all</em> of the region
              enclosed by the curve. Two of these fields blow up at the
              origin, which is inside the unit circle.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <strcmp strip="no">.*</strcmp>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Ask: is the field defined, with continuous partial
              derivatives, at <em>every</em> point of the disk bounded by
              the circle<mdash/>including the origin?
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
    <solution>
      <p>
        (a) <m>f(x,y) = a_1 xy</m>,
        <m>g(x,y) = a_2\arctan\!\left(x/y\right)</m> (so <m>C = a_2</m>,
        valid where <m>y \ne 0</m>), and
        <m>h(x,y) = a_3\sqrt{x^2+y^2}</m> (valid except at the origin),
        where <m>a_1, a_2, a_3</m> are the coefficients in
        <m>\mathbf{F}</m>, <m>\mathbf{G}</m>, <m>\mathbf{H}</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        (b) <m>\int_C \mathbf{F}\cdot d\mathbf{r} = 0</m> since
        <m>\mathbf{F}</m> is a gradient field on the whole plane and
        <m>C</m> is closed. On the unit circle <m>\mathbf{G}</m> is
        tangent, of length <m>a_2</m>, pointing clockwise, so
        <m>\int_C \mathbf{G}\cdot d\mathbf{r} = -2\pi a_2</m>.
        <m>\mathbf{H}</m> is radial, hence perpendicular to <m>C</m>, so
        its integral is <m>0</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        (c) Only <m>\mathbf{F}</m>: the domains of <m>\mathbf{G}</m> and
        <m>\mathbf{H}</m> do not include the origin, which is inside
        <m>C</m>, so Green's Theorem does not apply to them.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <!-- 11.  Circulation around a rectangle  (RP4 #11)                       -->
  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <exercise xml:id="ex-r4o-rect-circulation">
    <title>Circulation Around a Rectangle</title>
    <setup>
      <de-object name="a" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="1" max="5" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="x0" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="8" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="y0" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="1" max="6" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="curlF" context="formula">
        <de-expression>-a*x</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="circ" context="number">
        <de-expression>-a*x0^2*y0/2</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="posax" context="formula">
        <de-expression>a*x</de-expression>
      </de-object>
    </setup>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Use Green's Theorem to calculate the circulation of
        <m>\mathbf{F} = <eval obj="a"/>xy\,\mathbf{i}</m> around the
        rectangle <m>0 \le x \le <eval obj="x0"/></m>,
        <m>0 \le y \le <eval obj="y0"/></m>, oriented counterclockwise.
      </p>
      <p>
        With <m>\mathbf{F} = M\,\mathbf{i} + N\,\mathbf{j}</m>, first
        compute (as an expression in <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>):
        <m>\dfrac{\partial N}{\partial x}
        - \dfrac{\partial M}{\partial y} =</m>
        <fillin name="b1" mode="math" ansobj="curlF" width="10"/>
      </p>
      <p>
        circulation <m>=</m>
        <fillin name="b2" mode="number" ansobj="circ" width="10"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct: <m>N = 0</m>, so the integrand is
              <m>-\frac{\partial}{\partial y}\left(<eval obj="a"/>xy
              \right) = -<eval obj="a"/>x</m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <mathcmp obj="posax"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Sign error: the circulation form of Green's Theorem uses
              <m>N_x - M_y</m>, and here <m>M = <eval obj="a"/>xy</m> is
              the <m>\mathbf{i}</m>-component, so the integrand is
              <m>0 - <eval obj="a"/>x</m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Here <m>M = <eval obj="a"/>xy</m> and <m>N = 0</m>, so
              <m>N_x - M_y = -<eval obj="a"/>x</m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="circ.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(circ.value({})))" max="circ.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(circ.value({})))"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct:
              <m>\displaystyle\int_0^{<eval obj="x0"/>}\!\!
              \int_0^{<eval obj="y0"/>} \left(-<eval obj="a"/>x\right)
              dy\,dx
              = -<eval obj="a"/>\,\frac{\left(<eval obj="x0"/>\right)^2}
              {2}\left(<eval obj="y0"/>\right) = <eval obj="circ"/></m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              By Green's Theorem the circulation is
              <m>\iint_R\left(-<eval obj="a"/>x\right)dA</m> over the
              rectangle; integrating,
              <m>-<eval obj="a"/>\cdot
              \frac{\left(<eval obj="x0"/>\right)^2}{2}\cdot
              <eval obj="y0"/> = <eval obj="circ"/></m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
    <solution>
      <p>
        With <m>M = axy</m> and <m>N = 0</m>,
        <m>N_x - M_y = -ax</m>, so by Green's Theorem
      </p>
      <me>
        \oint_C \mathbf{F}\cdot d\mathbf{r}
        = \int_0^{x_0}\!\!\int_0^{y_0} (-ax)\,dy\,dx
        = -\frac{a\,x_0^2\,y_0}{2}.
      </me>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <!-- 12.  Circulation around a circle of radius m  (RP4 #12)              -->
  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <exercise xml:id="ex-r4o-circle-green">
    <title>Circulation Around a Circle</title>
    <setup>
      <de-object name="c1" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="8" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="c2" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="8" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="c4" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="8" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="u" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="0" max="1" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="d0" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="1" max="5" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="c3" context="number">
        <de-expression>c2+(2*u-1)*d0</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="m" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="6" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="diff" context="number">
        <de-expression>(2*u-1)*d0</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="circ" context="number">
        <de-expression>(2*u-1)*d0*pi*m^2</de-expression>
      </de-object>
    </setup>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Calculate
        <m>\displaystyle\int_C \left(\left(<eval obj="c1"/>x
        + <eval obj="c2"/>y\right)\mathbf{i}
        + \left(<eval obj="c3"/>x + <eval obj="c4"/>y\right)\mathbf{j}
        \right)\cdot d\mathbf{r}</m>, where <m>C</m> is the circular path
        with center <m>(a,b)</m> and radius <m><eval obj="m"/></m>,
        oriented counterclockwise. Use Green's Theorem.
      </p>
      <p>
        With <m>\mathbf{F} = M\,\mathbf{i} + N\,\mathbf{j}</m>, first
        compute (a number):
        <m>\dfrac{\partial N}{\partial x}
        - \dfrac{\partial M}{\partial y} =</m>
        <fillin name="b1" mode="number" ansobj="diff" width="6"/>
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\displaystyle\int_C \mathbf{F}\cdot d\mathbf{r} =</m>
        <fillin name="b2" mode="number" ansobj="circ" width="12"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="diff.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(diff.value({})))" max="diff.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(diff.value({})))"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct: <m>N_x - M_y = <eval obj="c3"/> -
              <eval obj="c2"/></m>, a constant.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              <m>N_x = \frac{\partial}{\partial x}\left(<eval obj="c3"/>x
              + <eval obj="c4"/>y\right) = <eval obj="c3"/></m> and
              <m>M_y = \frac{\partial}{\partial y}\left(<eval obj="c1"/>x
              + <eval obj="c2"/>y\right) = <eval obj="c2"/></m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="circ.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(circ.value({})))" max="circ.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(circ.value({})))"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct: the integrand is the constant
              <m><eval obj="diff"/></m>, so the integral is
              <m><eval obj="diff"/>\cdot\text{Area}
              = <eval obj="diff"/>\,\pi\left(<eval obj="m"/>\right)^2
              \approx <eval obj="circ"/></m><mdash/>independent of the
              center <m>(a,b)</m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              By Green's Theorem the integral equals
              <m>\left(N_x - M_y\right)</m> times the area of the disk,
              <m>\pi\left(<eval obj="m"/>\right)^2</m>. The center of the
              circle does not matter, because the integrand is constant.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
    <solution>
      <p>
        <m>N_x - M_y</m> is the constant difference of the two cross
        coefficients, so Green's Theorem gives
        <m>\left(N_x - M_y\right)\cdot\pi m^2</m>, where <m>m</m> is the
        radius; the center <m>(a,b)</m> plays no role.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <!-- 13.  A family of radial fields r^A (x i + y j)  (RP4 #13)            -->
  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <exercise xml:id="ex-r4o-radial-power">
    <title>Divergence of a Family of Radial Fields</title>
    <setup>
      <de-object name="pp" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="1" max="8" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="qq" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="1" max="6" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="a1" context="number">
        <de-expression>-2+pp</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="a2" context="number">
        <de-expression>-2-qq</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="coeff" context="formula">
        <de-expression>2+A</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="expo" context="formula">
        <de-expression>A</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="minustwo" context="number">
        <de-expression>-2</de-expression>
      </de-object>
    </setup>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>r = \left(x^2+y^2\right)^{1/2}</m> and consider the vector
        field <m>\mathbf{F} = r^A\!\left(x\,\mathbf{i} +
        y\,\mathbf{j}\right)</m>, where <m>r \ne 0</m> and <m>A</m> is a
        constant. <m>\mathbf{F}</m> has no <m>z</m>-component and is
        independent of <m>z</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        (a) Find <m>\nabla \cdot \left(r^A\!\left(x\,\mathbf{i}
        + y\,\mathbf{j}\right)\right)</m>, and show that it can be written
        in the form <m>\nabla \cdot \mathbf{F} = c\,r^{a}</m>. As
        expressions in <m>A</m>:
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>c =</m> <fillin name="b1" mode="math" ansobj="coeff" width="8"/>, and
        <m>a =</m> <fillin name="b2" mode="math" ansobj="expo" width="8"/>.
      </p>
      <p>
        (b) For what values of <m>A</m> is <m>\nabla \cdot \mathbf{F}</m>
        positive, negative, or zero?
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\nabla \cdot \mathbf{F}</m> is positive for <m>A \gt</m>
        <fillin name="b3" mode="number" ansobj="minustwo" width="6"/>,
        negative for <m>A \lt</m>
        <fillin name="b4" mode="number" ansobj="minustwo" width="6"/>, and
        zero for <m>A =</m>
        <fillin name="b5" mode="number" ansobj="minustwo" width="6"/>.
      </p>
      <p>
        (c) Suppose that <m>A = <eval obj="a1"/></m>. What is the sign of
        the flux out of a small sphere centered at <m>(1,1,1)</m>? Type
        <c>positive</c> or <c>negative</c>:
        <fillin name="b6" mode="string" answer="positive" width="10"/>
      </p>
      <p>
        If instead <m>A = <eval obj="a2"/></m>, what is the sign of the
        flux out of a small sphere centered at <m>(1,1,1)</m>?
        <fillin name="b7" mode="string" answer="negative" width="10"/>
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>(Be sure you can say how your answers in part (c) would change
        if the question were about a small sphere centered at
        <m>(0,0,0)</m>.)</em>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct: using <m>r_x = x/r</m> and <m>r_y = y/r</m>,
              <m>\nabla \cdot \mathbf{F}
              = 2r^A + A r^{A-1}\frac{x^2+y^2}{r} = (2+A)\,r^A</m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Differentiate with the product rule:
              <m>\frac{\partial}{\partial x}\left(r^A x\right)
              = r^A + A x r^{A-1} r_x</m>, with <m>r_x = x/r</m>, and
              similarly in <m>y</m>. Adding, the cross terms combine to
              <m>A r^{A-1}\cdot\frac{x^2+y^2}{r} = A r^A</m>, so the
              divergence is <m>(2+A)\,r^A</m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct: the divergence is <m>(2+A)\,r^{A}</m>, the same
              power of <m>r</m> as in the field.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              The two terms of the divergence are <m>2r^A</m> and
              <m>A r^A</m>; both carry the power <m>A</m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="minustwo.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(minustwo.value({})))" max="minustwo.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(minustwo.value({})))"/>
          <feedback><p>Correct.</p></feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Since <m>r^A \gt 0</m> for <m>r \ne 0</m>, the sign of
              <m>(2+A)\,r^A</m> is the sign of <m>2+A</m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="minustwo.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(minustwo.value({})))" max="minustwo.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(minustwo.value({})))"/>
          <feedback><p>Correct.</p></feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              The sign of the divergence is the sign of <m>2+A</m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="minustwo.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(minustwo.value({})))" max="minustwo.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(minustwo.value({})))"/>
          <feedback><p>Correct.</p></feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              <m>(2+A)\,r^A = 0</m> for <m>r \ne 0</m> exactly when the
              coefficient <m>2+A</m> vanishes.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <strcmp case="insensitive">positive</strcmp>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct: with <m>A = <eval obj="a1"/> \gt -2</m> the
              divergence is positive near <m>(1,1,1)</m>, and the flux out
              of a small sphere has the sign of the divergence at its
              center.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <strcmp strip="no">.*</strcmp>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Think of the divergence at a point as the flux density out
              of a small sphere centered there: the flux has the same sign
              as the divergence. Here <m>A = <eval obj="a1"/></m>, so
              compare <m>A</m> with <m>-2</m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <strcmp case="insensitive">negative</strcmp>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct: <m>A = <eval obj="a2"/> \lt -2</m>, so the
              divergence<mdash/>and with it the flux out of a small
              sphere away from the origin<mdash/>is negative. (For a
              sphere centered at <m>(0,0,0)</m> no conclusion can be
              drawn this way: the field is not defined at the origin.)
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <strcmp strip="no">.*</strcmp>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Compare <m>A = <eval obj="a2"/></m> with <m>-2</m>: the
              divergence has the sign of <m>2+A</m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
    <solution>
      <p>
        (a) With <m>r_x = x/r</m> and <m>r_y = y/r</m>, the product rule
        gives <m>\nabla \cdot \mathbf{F} = 2r^A + A r^{A-1}
        \left(\tfrac{x^2+y^2}{r}\right) = (2+A)\,r^A</m>, so
        <m>c = 2+A</m> and <m>a = A</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        (b) Since <m>r^A \gt 0</m>, the divergence is positive for
        <m>A \gt -2</m>, negative for <m>A \lt -2</m>, and zero for
        <m>A = -2</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        (c) The flux out of a small sphere has the sign of the divergence
        at its center, provided the sphere avoids the origin, where the
        field is undefined: positive when <m>A \gt -2</m>, negative when
        <m>A \lt -2</m>. For a sphere centered at the origin the
        calculation above says nothing, since the divergence is not
        defined there.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <!-- 14.  Cylinder between two slanted planes  (RP4 #14)                  -->
  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <exercise xml:id="ex-r4o-cylinder-band">
    <title>Flux Through a Slanted Cylinder Band</title>
    <setup>
      <de-object name="R" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="1" max="5" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="Rsq" context="number">
        <de-expression>R^2</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="z0" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="1" max="3" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="dz" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="6" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="z1" context="number">
        <de-expression>z0+dz</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="flux" context="number">
        <de-expression>2*pi*R^2*dz</de-expression>
      </de-object>
    </setup>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Compute the flux of
        <m>\mathbf{F} = x\,\mathbf{i} + y\,\mathbf{j} + z\,\mathbf{k}</m>
        through the surface of the cylinder
        <m>x^2 + y^2 = <eval obj="Rsq"/></m> bounded below by the plane
        <m>x + y + z = <eval obj="z0"/></m>, above by the plane
        <m>x + y + z = <eval obj="z1"/></m>, and
        <alert>oriented away from the <m>z</m>-axis</alert>. Note that we
        are only interested in the flux through the curved surface of the
        cylinder.
      </p>
      <p>
        On the cylinder, <m>\mathbf{F}\cdot\hat{\mathbf{n}} =</m>
        <fillin name="b1" mode="number" ansobj="R" width="6"/>
        (a number).
      </p>
      <p>
        Flux <m>=</m> <fillin name="b2" mode="number" ansobj="flux" width="12"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="R.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(R.value({})))" max="R.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(R.value({})))"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct: the outward normal is
              <m>\hat{\mathbf{n}} = \frac{1}{<eval obj="R"/>}\langle x, y,
              0\rangle</m>, so <m>\mathbf{F}\cdot\hat{\mathbf{n}}
              = \frac{x^2+y^2}{<eval obj="R"/>}
              = \frac{<eval obj="Rsq"/>}{<eval obj="R"/>}
              = <eval obj="R"/></m> everywhere on the cylinder.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              The outward unit normal of the cylinder (away from the
              <m>z</m>-axis) is <m>\hat{\mathbf{n}} = \langle x, y, 0\rangle /
              <eval obj="R"/></m>; dot it with <m>\mathbf{F}</m> and use
              <m>x^2 + y^2 = <eval obj="Rsq"/></m>. The <m>z</m>-component
              of <m>\mathbf{F}</m> contributes nothing.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="flux.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(flux.value({})))" max="flux.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(flux.value({})))"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct: since <m>\mathbf{F}\cdot\hat{\mathbf{n}}</m> is constant,
              the flux is <m><eval obj="R"/>\cdot\text{Area}</m>. Above
              each point of the circle the band runs between the two
              slanted planes, a vertical gap of
              <m><eval obj="z1"/> - <eval obj="z0"/> = <eval obj="dz"/></m>,
              so the area is <m>2\pi\left(<eval obj="R"/>\right)
              \left(<eval obj="dz"/>\right)</m> and the flux is
              <m>2\pi\left(<eval obj="R"/>\right)^2
              \left(<eval obj="dz"/>\right) \approx <eval obj="flux"/></m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Parametrize the band by
              <m>\left(<eval obj="R"/>\cos t, <eval obj="R"/>\sin t,
              s\right)</m> with
              <m><eval obj="z0"/> - <eval obj="R"/>\cos t
              - <eval obj="R"/>\sin t \le s \le
              <eval obj="z1"/> - <eval obj="R"/>\cos t
              - <eval obj="R"/>\sin t</m>: for every <m>t</m> the
              <m>s</m>-interval has the same length
              <m><eval obj="dz"/></m>. With
              <m>\mathbf{F}\cdot\hat{\mathbf{n}} = <eval obj="R"/></m> constant,
              the flux is
              <m><eval obj="R"/>\cdot 2\pi <eval obj="R"/>\cdot
              <eval obj="dz"/> \approx <eval obj="flux"/></m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
    <solution>
      <p>
        On the cylinder of radius <m>R</m> the outward normal is
        <m>\langle x, y, 0\rangle / R</m>, so
        <m>\mathbf{F}\cdot\hat{\mathbf{n}} = \left(x^2+y^2\right)/R = R</m>: the
        <m>z</m>-component of <m>\mathbf{F}</m> is tangent to the surface.
        The two slanted planes cut every vertical line on the cylinder in
        a segment of the same length <m>\Delta z = z_1 - z_0</m>, so the
        band has area <m>2\pi R\,\Delta z</m>, and
      </p>
      <me>
        \text{Flux} = R\cdot 2\pi R\,\Delta z = 2\pi R^2\,\Delta z.
      </me>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <!-- 15.  Flux out of a closed cone  (RP4 #15)                            -->
  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <exercise xml:id="ex-r4o-cone">
    <title>Flux Out of a Closed Cone</title>
    <setup>
      <de-object name="a" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="5" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="B" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="5" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="C" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="5" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="r" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="1" max="3" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="divF" context="formula">
        <de-expression>2*a*y</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="flux" context="number">
        <de-expression>a*r^4*pi/2</de-expression>
      </de-object>
    </setup>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find the flux of
        <m>\mathbf{F} = <eval obj="B"/>z\,\mathbf{i}
        + <eval obj="a"/>y^2\,\mathbf{j} + <eval obj="C"/>x\,\mathbf{k}</m>
        out of the closed cone <m>y = \sqrt{x^2 + z^2}</m>, with
        <m>0 \le y \le <eval obj="r"/></m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\nabla \cdot \mathbf{F} =</m>
        <fillin name="b1" mode="math" ansobj="divF" width="10"/>
        (an expression in <m>x</m>, <m>y</m>, <m>z</m>)
      </p>
      <p>
        flux <m>=</m> <fillin name="b2" mode="number" ansobj="flux" width="12"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct: only the middle component depends on its own
              variable, so <m>\nabla \cdot \mathbf{F}
              = 2\!\left(<eval obj="a"/>\right)y</m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              <m>\frac{\partial}{\partial x}\left(<eval obj="B"/>z\right)
              = 0</m>,
              <m>\frac{\partial}{\partial y}\left(<eval obj="a"/>y^2
              \right) = 2\!\left(<eval obj="a"/>\right)y</m>, and
              <m>\frac{\partial}{\partial z}\left(<eval obj="C"/>x\right)
              = 0</m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="flux.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(flux.value({})))" max="flux.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(flux.value({})))"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct: in cylindrical coordinates around the
              <m>y</m>-axis the interior of the cone is
              <m>0 \le \rho \le y \le <eval obj="r"/></m>. Integrating
              <m>\rho</m> first,
              <m>\int_0^{y} 2\!\left(<eval obj="a"/>\right)
              y\,\rho\,d\rho = <eval obj="a"/>y^3</m>, and then
              <m>2\pi\int_0^{<eval obj="r"/>} <eval obj="a"/>y^3\,dy
              = \frac{<eval obj="a"/>\left(<eval obj="r"/>\right)^4\pi}
              {2} \approx <eval obj="flux"/></m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Apply the Divergence Theorem and use cylindrical coordinates
              with <m>y</m> playing the role of the axis: the interior of
              the cone is <m>0 \le \theta \le 2\pi</m>,
              <m>0 \le \rho \le y</m>, <m>0 \le y \le <eval obj="r"/></m>.
              Then
              <m>\iiint_W 2\!\left(<eval obj="a"/>\right)y\,dV
              = 2\pi\int_0^{<eval obj="r"/>} <eval obj="a"/>y^3\,dy
              = \frac{<eval obj="a"/>\left(<eval obj="r"/>\right)^4\pi}
              {2}</m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
    <solution>
      <p>
        <m>\nabla \cdot \mathbf{F} = 2ay</m>, where <m>a</m> is the
        coefficient of <m>y^2</m>. By the Divergence Theorem, using
        cylindrical coordinates around the <m>y</m>-axis (the cone is
        <m>0 \le \rho \le y \le r</m>),
      </p>
      <me>
        \text{flux} = \int_0^{2\pi}\!\!\int_0^{r}\!\!\int_0^{y}
        2ay\,\rho\,d\rho\,dy\,d\theta
        = 2\pi\int_0^{r} a y^3\,dy = \frac{a r^4 \pi}{2}.
      </me>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <!-- 16.  Divergence theorem on a tetrahedron  (RP4 #16)                  -->
  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <exercise xml:id="ex-r4o-tetrahedron">
    <title>Flux Through the Surface of a Tetrahedron</title>
    <setup>
      <de-object name="a" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="4" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="b" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="1" max="5" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="c" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="1" max="5" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="divF" context="formula">
        <de-expression>1+x</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="flux" context="number">
        <de-expression>a*b*c*(a+4)/24</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="vol" context="number">
        <de-expression>a*b*c/6</de-expression>
      </de-object>
    </setup>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Use the Divergence Theorem to calculate the flux of
        <m>\mathbf{F}</m> across <m>S</m>, where
        <m>\mathbf{F} = z\,\mathbf{i} + y\,\mathbf{j} +
        zx\,\mathbf{k}</m> and <m>S</m> is the surface of the tetrahedron
        enclosed by the coordinate planes and the plane
      </p>
      <me>
        \frac{x}{<eval obj="a"/>} + \frac{y}{<eval obj="b"/>}
        + \frac{z}{<eval obj="c"/>} = 1.
      </me>
      <p>
        <m>\nabla \cdot \mathbf{F} =</m>
        <fillin name="b1" mode="math" ansobj="divF" width="10"/>
        (an expression in <m>x</m>, <m>y</m>, <m>z</m>)
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\displaystyle\iint_S \mathbf{F}\cdot\hat{\mathbf{n}}\,d\sigma =</m>
        <fillin name="b2" mode="number" ansobj="flux" width="12"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct: <m>\frac{\partial z}{\partial x} = 0</m>,
              <m>\frac{\partial y}{\partial y} = 1</m>,
              <m>\frac{\partial (zx)}{\partial z} = x</m>, so
              <m>\nabla \cdot \mathbf{F} = 1 + x</m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Differentiate each component with respect to its own
              variable: the first gives <m>0</m>, the second <m>1</m>,
              and the third <m>x</m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="flux.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(flux.value({})))" max="flux.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(flux.value({})))"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct:
              <m>\displaystyle\iiint_E (1+x)\,dV
              = \frac{<eval obj="a"/>\cdot<eval obj="b"/>\cdot
              <eval obj="c"/>\left(<eval obj="a"/>+4\right)}{24}
              \approx <eval obj="flux"/></m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp object="vol" min="vol.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(vol.value({})))" max="vol.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(vol.value({})))"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              That is the volume of the tetrahedron, which is
              <m>\iiint_E 1\,dV</m><mdash/>but the integrand is
              <m>1 + x</m>, so the <m>x</m>-term still needs to be
              integrated.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Set up the triple integral over the tetrahedron with
              <m>0 \le x \le <eval obj="a"/></m>,
              <m>0 \le y \le <eval obj="b"/>\left(1 -
              \tfrac{x}{<eval obj="a"/>}\right)</m>,
              <m>0 \le z \le <eval obj="c"/>\left(1 -
              \tfrac{x}{<eval obj="a"/>} -
              \tfrac{y}{<eval obj="b"/>}\right)</m>. Integrating
              <m>1 + x</m> gives
              <m>\tfrac{1}{2}<eval obj="b"/>\cdot<eval obj="c"/>
              \int_0^{<eval obj="a"/>} (1+x)\left(1 -
              \tfrac{x}{<eval obj="a"/>}\right)^2 dx
              = \frac{<eval obj="a"/>\cdot<eval obj="b"/>\cdot
              <eval obj="c"/>\left(<eval obj="a"/>+4\right)}{24}
              \approx <eval obj="flux"/></m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
    <solution>
      <p>
        <m>\nabla \cdot \mathbf{F} = 0 + 1 + x = 1 + x</m>. The
        tetrahedron <m>E</m> has vertices <m>(0,0,0)</m>,
        <m>(a,0,0)</m>, <m>(0,b,0)</m>, <m>(0,0,c)</m>, so
      </p>
      <me>
        \iint_S \mathbf{F}\cdot\hat{\mathbf{n}}\,d\sigma
        = \iiint_E (1+x)\,dV
        = \frac{bc}{2}\int_0^{a}(1+x)\left(1-\frac{x}{a}\right)^2 dx
        = \frac{abc\,(a+4)}{24},
      </me>
      <p>
        using <m>\int_0^{a}(1+x)\left(1-\tfrac{x}{a}\right)^2 dx
        = \tfrac{a}{3} + \tfrac{a^2}{12} = \tfrac{a(a+4)}{12}</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  </exercises>
</section>
